PRD: Expiring Deployments (Expiring Helm Installs & Namespaces)

1) Summary

Expiring Deployments is a reusable, installable Helm chart that deploys a Kubernetes CronJob to automatically clean up temporary environments by:
	•	Deleting expired namespaces (Namespace TTL)
	•	Uninstalling expired Helm releases (Helm TTL)

It is designed to be:
	•	Safe-by-default (opt-in metadata, dry-run default, protected namespaces)
	•	Reusable across clusters
	•	Easy to adopt (simple annotations + small Helm helper template)

⸻

2) Problem Statement

Teams create temporary environments (preview, QA, sandbox) and forget to remove them. This causes:
	•	Unnecessary cluster spend (compute + storage + IPs + LB costs)
	•	Resource clutter (namespaces, services, ingresses, secrets)
	•	Operational risk (stale workloads, outdated configs, noisy alerts)
	•	Manual cleanup overhead and inconsistent practices

We need a standard, automated cleanup mechanism that scales across teams and clusters.

⸻

3) Goals ✅
	1.	Single install per cluster via Helm (“installable CronJob”)
	2.	Support two cleanup modes:
	•	Namespace TTL: delete namespaces past TTL
	•	Helm TTL: uninstall Helm releases past TTL
	3.	Metadata-driven opt-in
	4.	Safe-by-default:
	•	dry-run mode
	•	protected namespaces
	•	allowlist/selector support
	•	clear audit logs
	5.	Simple adoption for CI/CD and chart authors

⸻

4) Non-Goals ❌
	•	Not a general “garbage collector” for arbitrary unlabeled resources
	•	Not responsible for cleaning external cloud resources (RDS, S3, etc.)
	•	Not handling complicated “data retention policies” for PVCs beyond namespace deletion behavior
	•	Not a replacement for GitOps “desired state” cleanup (though compatible)

⸻

5) Users & Stakeholders 👥

Primary users
	•	Developers using preview environments
	•	CI/CD pipelines that create ephemeral installs

Stakeholders
	•	Platform/SRE (operability, safety, cluster hygiene)
	•	Security (least privilege, preventing accidental deletion)
	•	Finance/Leadership (cost control)

⸻

6) Use Cases

A) Preview environment per PR (namespace-per-preview)
	•	CI creates namespace pr-1234
	•	Annotates namespace with TTL
	•	Expiring Deployments deletes the namespace after TTL (or after PR is merged + TTL)

B) Ephemeral Helm install in a shared namespace
	•	Teams deploy to dev-sandboxes namespace
	•	Release includes a TTL “lease marker” resource
	•	Expiring Deployments uninstalls the Helm release after TTL

C) Ad-hoc manual sandbox
	•	Engineer installs chart with TTL flags/values
	•	No follow-up needed; it cleans itself

⸻

7) Product Requirements

7.1 Functional Requirements

Namespace TTL
	•	Identify namespaces opt-in via annotation (enabled=true)
	•	Compute expiration based on TTL
	•	Delete expired namespaces
	•	Skip protected namespaces always

Helm TTL
	•	Identify Helm releases opt-in via a lease/marker resource created by the release
	•	Compute expiration based on TTL
	•	Uninstall expired releases
	•	Skip protected namespaces always

Shared logic
	•	TTL derived from metadata:
	•	expires-at (absolute time) OR
	•	ttl-hours (relative time from created-at / creationTimestamp fallback)
	•	Clear logs: what was evaluated, what was deleted, and why
	•	Dry-run mode: logs actions without deleting anything

7.2 Non-Functional Requirements
	•	Idempotent: running multiple times doesn’t cause harm
	•	Safe: never touches protected namespaces
	•	Configurable: allowlist/selector so it only manages intended namespaces
	•	Reliable: handles malformed metadata gracefully (skip + log)
	•	Auditable: emits clear per-run summary logs

⸻

8) Success Metrics 📈
	•	≥ 90% reduction in orphaned preview namespaces within 30 days
	•	≥ 99% CronJob run success rate (excluding intentional skips)
	•	0 accidental deletions of protected namespaces
	•	Measurable drop in non-prod cluster cost/usage (CPU/mem counts, #namespaces)

