Tech Spec: Expiring Deployments Helm Chart

1) Overview

Expiring Deployments is delivered as a Helm chart that installs:
	•	CronJob (runs periodically)
	•	ServiceAccount
	•	ClusterRole / ClusterRoleBinding
	•	ConfigMap for configuration (optional; values env is also fine)

The job runs a TTL evaluation loop:
	1.	Scan namespaces → delete expired namespaces
	2.	Scan Helm “lease markers” → uninstall expired Helm releases
	3.	Print a run summary (counts, actions, errors)

⸻

2) Why Helm “lease marker” instead of reading Helm release metadata?

Helm itself does not provide a stable, user-controlled place to attach arbitrary TTL annotations that are reliably available via helm list.

So we implement Helm TTL by requiring a marker resource (recommended: ConfigMap) created as part of the release, containing TTL metadata.

This is:
	•	Simple
	•	Visible in Kubernetes
	•	Works across Helm upgrades
	•	Easy to query with label selectors

⸻

3) Metadata Contract (Namespace + Helm Lease Marker)

Use an annotation prefix you control (recommended best practice). Below uses expiring-deployments.io/* as a placeholder — swap to your org domain if desired.

3.1 Required opt-in annotation
	•	expiring-deployments.io/enabled: "true"

3.2 Expiration inputs (choose one)

Option A: relative TTL
	•	expiring-deployments.io/ttl-hours: "24"
	•	Optional:
	•	expiring-deployments.io/created-at: "2026-01-18T10:00:00Z" (RFC3339)

If created-at missing, fallback to the resource’s metadata.creationTimestamp.

Option B: absolute expiration
	•	expiring-deployments.io/expires-at: "2026-01-20T00:00:00Z" (RFC3339)

3.3 Recommended governance metadata
	•	expiring-deployments.io/owner: "team-name"
	•	expiring-deployments.io/purpose: "preview|sandbox|qa"
	•	expiring-deployments.io/source: "github-pr-1234"

These are useful for safety & audit logs.

⸻

4) Helm TTL Marker Resource

4.1 Resource type

Use a namespaced ConfigMap created by the release:

Template example (to be embedded into app charts that want Helm TTL):

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: {{ printf "%s-ttl-lease" .Release.Name | trunc 63 | trimSuffix "-" }}
  labels:
    expiring-deployments.io/type: helm-release
    app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name | quote }}
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: "Helm"
  annotations:
    expiring-deployments.io/enabled: "true"
    expiring-deployments.io/ttl-hours: {{ .Values.cleanup.ttlHours | default 24 | quote }}
    # created-at optional; creationTimestamp fallback works fine
    expiring-deployments.io/owner: {{ .Values.cleanup.owner | default "unknown" | quote }}
data:
  note: "TTL lease marker for Helm release cleanup"

4.2 How Expiring Deployments maps marker → release
	•	Namespace comes from the marker’s namespace
	•	Release name from label: app.kubernetes.io/instance

⸻

5) Cleanup Precedence Rules
	1.	Namespace deletion wins
	•	If a namespace is expired and scheduled for deletion, skip Helm uninstalls in that namespace (avoids noisy failures and wasted work).
	2.	If only a Helm lease marker is expired:
	•	Uninstall the release in that namespace.

⸻

6) Cleanup Algorithm

6.1 Namespace Sweep
	1.	List namespaces
	2.	Filter:
	•	expiring-deployments.io/enabled == "true"
	•	namespace name not in protectedNamespaces
	•	matches allowlist selectors (regex/labels)
	3.	Compute expiry:
	•	If expires-at: parse RFC3339 → expiry
	•	Else:
	•	baseTime = created-at OR creationTimestamp
	•	expiry = baseTime + ttlHours
	4.	If expired:
	•	if dryRun: log “would delete”
	•	else: DELETE /api/v1/namespaces/{name}

6.2 Helm Lease Sweep
	1.	List ConfigMaps across namespaces with label selector:
	•	expiring-deployments.io/type=helm-release
	2.	Filter:
	•	annotation enabled
	•	namespace not protected
	•	namespace allowlisted
	•	namespace not already scheduled for deletion
	3.	Compute expiry same rules as above
	4.	If expired:
	•	helm uninstall <release> -n <namespace> --wait --timeout <X>

⸻

7) Helm Chart Design

7.1 Chart contents
	•	templates/cronjob.yaml
	•	templates/serviceaccount.yaml
	•	templates/rbac.yaml
	•	values.yaml
	•	(optional) templates/configmap-config.yaml

7.2 CronJob settings (recommended defaults)
	•	concurrencyPolicy: Forbid
	•	startingDeadlineSeconds: 600
	•	successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 1
	•	failedJobsHistoryLimit: 3
	•	activeDeadlineSeconds: 900 (or configurable)

⸻

8) Configuration (values.yaml)

Recommended core values:

schedule: "0 * * * *"   # hourly
dryRun: true

features:
  namespaces: true
  helmReleases: true

selectors:
  managedNamespaceRegex: "^pr-|^preview-|^sandbox-"
  managedNamespaceLabels: {} # optional

safety:
  protectedNamespaces:
    - kube-system
    - kube-public
    - default
    - prod
    - production
  maxDeletionsPerRun: 20        # circuit breaker (recommended)
  requireOwnerAnnotation: false # optional hardening

helm:
  uninstallTimeout: "5m"
  uninstallWait: true


⸻

9) RBAC Model 🔐

9.1 Namespace TTL RBAC (cluster scope required)
	•	get, list, delete on namespaces

9.2 Helm TTL RBAC (important caveat)

Uninstalling a Helm release means deleting multiple namespaced resources. That requires the CronJob service account to have delete permissions in the target namespaces for the resource kinds your charts install.

Recommended approach:
	•	Use allowlisted namespaces and grant permissions only in those namespaces (best practice),
OR use a cluster-wide role in non-prod clusters where risk is acceptable.

At minimum, the SA also needs:
	•	get, list configmaps (to find lease markers)
	•	access to Helm release storage objects:
	•	usually secrets (sh.helm.release.v1.*) or configmaps depending on Helm storage driver
	•	so: get, list (and sometimes delete) on secrets/configmaps in managed namespaces

Design choice in this spec
	•	Provide a default ClusterRole suitable for non-prod
	•	Provide an optional “restricted mode” where user supplies a list of namespaces and we create RoleBindings per namespace

⸻

10) Implementation Language & Container Image

Recommendation: Implement cleanup logic as a small Go CLI

Reason: time parsing + JSON parsing + reliable behavior across distros is much safer than shell + date.

Container strategy
	•	Minimal image containing:
	•	expiring-deployments binary
	•	helm binary (or use Helm SDK later)

Phase 2 enhancement
	•	Use Helm SDK (Go) to uninstall releases without shelling out to helm

⸻

11) Observability 📣

Logs
	•	Per run:
	•	scanned namespaces count
	•	scanned leases count
	•	deletions attempted/succeeded/failed
	•	dry-run mode indicator
	•	Per deletion:
	•	timestamp, resource type, name/namespace, expiry calculation inputs, reason

Optional future
	•	Emit Kubernetes Events for deletes
	•	Push metrics to Pushgateway (CronJob-friendly)

⸻

12) Error Handling & Retry Behavior
	•	If metadata invalid:
	•	Skip + log warning (do not delete)
	•	If delete fails:
	•	Log error; allow CronJob retry next schedule
	•	If helm uninstall fails:
	•	Log error; keep lease marker so it retries later
	•	Circuit breaker:
	•	maxDeletionsPerRun stops runaway deletion due to misconfig

⸻

